Macridis demanded explanation
The “traditional approach” was criticized as:

parochial—centered on Europe;
descriptive—missing the why question;
monographic—focused narrowly on formal institutions;
not genuinely comparative—countries appeared in separate silos; and
static—unable to explain political change.



This critique helped shift the field from describing governments to explaining political behavior and change.


Suggested time: 2.5 minutes. Ask which criticism is still most relevant to student research today.
[Sources] - Ishiyama (2012), discussion of Macridis (1955).

The behavioral revolution changed the unit of analysis
Behavioralists emphasized:

theory joined to empirical observation;
surveys, content analysis, statistics, history, and qualitative evidence;
insights from economics, sociology, and psychology;
common concepts such as power, political system, and democracy; and
an aspiration toward value-free science.



The field moved beyond constitutions on paper to study what political actors actually do.


Suggested time: 2.5 minutes. Mention the Chicago School and its students as a bridge to the postwar behavioral revolution.
[Sources] - Ishiyama (2012), behavioral-revolution discussion.
